# Domain 1.0 Network Fundamentals: Detailed Notes with Analogies

Each topic has the idea, the facts the exam tests, and an analogy to hold it in memory. Weight: 20% of the exam.

---

## 1. Network components

Router connects *different* networks and forwards packets by destination IP address using its routing table.
- Analogy: the sorting hub of a courier company. It reads the destination address on each parcel and picks the next road toward it.

L2 switch connects devices inside *one* LAN and forwards frames by MAC address.
- Analogy: the receptionist of a single building. She knows which desk (port) each employee (MAC) sits at, but she can't send mail to another city.

L3 switch is a switch that can also route between VLANs, at hardware speed.
- Analogy: the same receptionist, now able to carry mail between floors (VLANs) without calling the post office.

Firewall / NGFW allows or blocks traffic using rules. A *stateful* firewall remembers connections, so return traffic is allowed automatically. A *next-generation* firewall also understands applications and users.
- Analogy: a gate guard with a guest list who remembers who went out, so the visitor can come back in.

IPS (intrusion prevention system) inspects traffic for attack patterns and blocks it *inline*. An IDS only alerts.
- Analogy: the airport scanner that stops the passenger (IPS), versus a CCTV camera that only rings an alarm (IDS).

Access point (AP) bridges wireless clients onto the wired LAN.
- Analogy: a doorway between the air and the cable.

Controllers manage many devices from one place.
- A WLC (wireless LAN controller) manages lightweight APs.
- Cisco Catalyst Center (formerly DNA Center) automates and monitors wired and wireless networks.
- Analogy: head office pushing one policy to every branch, or an orchestra conductor.

Endpoints and servers: endpoints are PCs, phones and IoT devices that use the network. Servers provide services.
- Analogy: customers and the kitchen.

PoE (Power over Ethernet) carries power and data on one cable.
- 802.3af: 15.4 W. 802.3at (PoE+): 30 W. 802.3bt: up to 60 W or 100 W.
- Analogy: a single cable on a CCTV pole that brings both the internet and the electricity.

Memory hook: Router = *between* networks. Switch = *inside* a network. Firewall = *rules*. IPS = *rules plus attack patterns, blocking*.

---

## 2. Topologies

2-tier (collapsed core) has an access layer and a combined distribution/core layer. It suits small and medium campuses.
- Analogy: a small bank branch where the tellers' counters report straight to one regional manager.

3-tier has access, distribution and core layers. It suits large campuses.
- Access connects users.
- Distribution applies policy and aggregates.
- Core moves traffic fast and does nothing else.
- Analogy: a city's roads. Local streets lead to district roads, which lead to highways. Highways just move traffic fast.

Spine-leaf is the data center design. Every leaf switch connects to every spine switch. Leaves never connect to each other, and neither do spines.
- Any server-to-server path is predictable: leaf, spine, leaf.
- Analogy: a metro system where every neighborhood station connects to every central interchange. Any two neighborhoods are always exactly two hops apart.

WAN connects sites in different locations. Options include leased line, MPLS, metro Ethernet and Internet VPN.
- Analogy: highways and airline routes between cities that you rent from a carrier, because you don't own the roads.

SOHO (small office/home office) usually has one box that acts as router, switch, firewall and AP.
- Analogy: a studio apartment where the kitchen, bedroom and living room are all one room.

On-prem vs cloud
- On-prem: you own and run everything. Analogy: owning a house, where repairs are your problem.
- Cloud: you rent capacity from a provider and pay as you use it. Analogy: renting a hotel room, where the hotel handles maintenance.
- Hybrid combines both.
- Cloud service models: IaaS is renting the land and building, PaaS is a furnished flat, SaaS is a hotel stay where you just use it.

