## 3. Cabling, interface errors, duplex and speed

Copper (UTP): Cat5e or Cat6 with an RJ-45 connector, up to 100 m. It can pick up electrical interference.
- Analogy: a water pipe. It is cheap and works well, but it leaks (interference) over distance.

Fiber: carries light and is immune to electrical interference.
- Single-mode: thin core, laser, very long distance (kilometers), more expensive. Analogy: a laser pointer down a narrow straw. There is one clean path, so it travels far.
- Multimode: wider core, LED or VCSEL, hundreds of meters, cheaper. Analogy: a flashlight in a wide pipe. The light bounces along many paths and spreads, so the distance is shorter.

Shared media vs point-to-point
- Old hubs shared one wire, so devices took turns.
- Switch ports are dedicated links.
- Analogy: a party-line phone versus a private line.

Interface errors (check with `show interfaces`):

| Error | Meaning | Usual cause |
|---|---|---|
| Collisions | Frames crashed on a shared link | Half-duplex only |
| Late collisions | Collision after the first 64 bytes | Duplex mismatch or cable too long |
| CRC errors | Corrupted frames | Bad cable, interference, mismatch |
| Runts | Frames under 64 bytes | Collisions, faulty NIC |
| Giants | Frames over 1518 bytes | Misconfigured MTU, faulty NIC |

Duplex: full-duplex means both sides send and receive at the same time. Half-duplex means one at a time.
- Analogy: full duplex is a phone call. Half duplex is a walkie-talkie where you say "over".
- Duplex mismatch: one side thinks phone call and the other thinks walkie-talkie. The result is late collisions and a link that works but is very slow.

Speed mismatch: the link usually stays down.
- Analogy: two people who speak different languages.

Fix: use auto-negotiation on both ends, or set both ends manually to the same values.

