## 4. TCP vs UDP

---------------------------------------------------------------------
|       | TCP                | UDP                                   |
|-------|--------------------|---------------------------------------|
| Type  | Connection-oriented | Connectionless |
| Setup | 3-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) | None |
| Reliability | Sequencing, acknowledgments, retransmission, flow control | None |
| Header | 20 bytes minimum | 8 bytes |
| Used for | HTTP/HTTPS, SSH, Telnet, FTP, SMTP | DNS, DHCP, TFTP, SNMP, syslog, NTP, voice, video |
---------------------------------------------------------------------

- TCP analogy: registered courier with signature. First you confirm someone is home ("Are you there? Yes. OK, delivering"), then each parcel is acknowledged, and lost ones are re-sent.
- UDP analogy: dropping a flyer in a letterbox, or a live radio broadcast. It is fast and has no confirmation, so a lost flyer is simply lost.
- Ports: the IP address is the building, and the port is the apartment number.

Ports to memorize: FTP 20/21, TFTP 69 (UDP), SSH 22, Telnet 23, SMTP 25, DNS 53, DHCP 67/68 (UDP), syslog 514 (UDP)  HTTP 80, HTTPS 443, NTP 123 (UDP), SNMP 161/162 (UDP).

