## 6. IPv6 addressing and address types

Structure: 128 bits, written as eight groups of four hex digits separated by colons.

Shortening rules:
1. Drop leading zeros in each group.
2. Replace one run of all-zero groups with `::`, and only once per address.
- Example: `2001:0DB8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001` becomes `2001:DB8::1`.

Other facts:
- LAN prefix is normally /64, so the last 64 bits are the interface ID.
- EUI-64 builds the interface ID from the MAC address: split the MAC, insert FFFE in the middle, and flip the 7th bit.
- IPv6 has no broadcast. It uses multicast, and NDP replaces ARP.
- Analogy for the size: IPv4 is a phone system that ran out of numbers. IPv6 has so many that every grain of sand could get one.

Address types:

| Type | Prefix | Analogy |
|---|---|---|
| Global unicast | 2000::/3 | Your public postal address; anyone can reach you |
| Unique local | FC00::/7 | Internal company extension, private |
| Link-local | FE80::/10 | Your neighbors in the same lane; you can shout to them but not beyond |
| Multicast | FF00::/8 | A group chat; only members receive |
| Anycast | (a unicast address shared by many) | A hotline to the *nearest* pizza outlet; the closest branch answers |
| Loopback | ::1 | Talking to yourself |

Key multicast addresses: FF02::1 is all nodes, FF02::2 is all routers.

Memory hook: every IPv6 interface *automatically* gets a link-local address (FE80::), and routers use it as the next hop.

