## 9. Virtualization (VMs, containers, VRFs)

Hypervisor is the software that splits one physical machine into many virtual ones.
- Type 1 (bare metal): runs directly on hardware (ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM).
- Type 2 (hosted): runs on top of an OS (VirtualBox, VMware Workstation).

Virtual machine (VM) has its own full operating system.
- Analogy: a full separate flat in an apartment building, with its own kitchen and bathroom (its own OS). It is heavy but strongly isolated.

Container shares the host's OS kernel and packages only the app and its libraries.
- Analogy: a private room in a shared flat. It has its own bed and locker but shares the kitchen. It is light and starts in seconds.
- Docker builds and runs containers. Kubernetes manages many of them.

VRF (Virtual Routing and Forwarding) gives one router *multiple independent routing tables*.
- Analogy: one shopping mall with separate wings. Two shops can both be "Shop 101" in different wings, which means overlapping IP ranges are possible.
- Used for multi-tenant and segmented networks.

